1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:07,647
Ask a chatbot how many r's are in strawberry, and for years the answer was a coin flip.

2
00:00:07,927 --> 00:00:09,799
Here is why.

3
00:00:10,079 --> 00:00:13,847
This is that exact question, as the model actually receives it.

4
00:00:14,126 --> 00:00:15,998
The coloured chips are tokens.

5
00:00:16,279 --> 00:00:19,254
Look at the word strawberry: it is a single chip.

6
00:00:19,535 --> 00:00:24,791
The model never sees the letters s, t, r, a, w.

7
00:00:25,071 --> 00:00:29,151
It sees one unit, and it has to remember how that unit is spelled.

8
00:00:29,431 --> 00:00:34,063
I built this tool with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

9
00:00:34,863 --> 00:00:38,726
Language models don't read characters and they don't read words.

10
00:00:39,006 --> 00:00:42,918
They read tokens: chunks of text from a fixed vocabulary.

11
00:00:43,198 --> 00:00:49,631
A token can be a whole word, part of a word, a space, a number fragment, or a single symbol.

12
00:00:49,911 --> 00:00:51,783
Why chunks?

13
00:00:52,063 --> 00:00:53,935
Because it's a compromise.

14
00:00:54,215 --> 00:00:56,999
Characters would make every text very long.

15
00:00:57,279 --> 00:01:01,839
Whole words would need a gigantic vocabulary and couldn't handle new words.

16
00:01:02,119 --> 00:01:03,991
Chunks sit in between.

17
00:01:04,271 --> 00:01:07,247
And tokens are the unit of everything practical.

18
00:01:07,527 --> 00:01:09,399
You pay per token.

19
00:01:09,679 --> 00:01:12,607
The context window is measured in tokens.

20
00:01:12,887 --> 00:01:18,743
And what the model can and cannot do with spelling and arithmetic follows from how the text was chopped up.

21
00:01:19,543 --> 00:01:25,854
The tokenizers themselves come from an open-source library called tiktoken, which OpenAI publishes.

22
00:01:26,134 --> 00:01:28,511
The app doesn't call any API..

23
00:01:28,791 --> 00:01:32,439
Encoding turns the text into a list of integer IDs.

24
00:01:32,719 --> 00:01:38,934
Then each ID is turned back into the bytes it stands for, so we can show the piece of text behind it.

25
00:01:39,214 --> 00:01:41,927
Those integers are all the model ever gets..

26
00:01:42,207 --> 00:01:50,439
To draw them, each piece becomes a little coloured span, cycling through six colours so neighbouring tokens are easy to tell apart.

27
00:01:50,719 --> 00:01:53,214
Hover over a chip and you see its ID.

28
00:01:53,494 --> 00:02:06,910
The app does this for two vocabularies at once: o200k, used by the GPT-4o family, and cl100k, used by GPT-4 and GPT-3.5.

29
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:15,439
The sentence How many r's are in strawberry has thirty-one characters and comes out as eight tokens, in both vocabularies.

30
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:22,751
How, many, r, an apostrophe s, are, in, strawberry, and the question mark.

31
00:02:23,031 --> 00:02:27,759
Open the token IDs and you see the real thing: just a list of integers.

32
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:30,103
The word strawberry is one of them.

33
00:02:30,383 --> 00:02:34,895
To count its r's, the model can't look at letters, because there aren't any.

34
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:38,799
It has to have learned the spelling of that token from its training text.

35
00:02:39,079 --> 00:02:42,535
That is a harder job, and the reason letter counting went wrong.

36
00:02:43,335 --> 00:02:45,207
Now numbers.

37
00:02:45,487 --> 00:02:50,215
The year two thousand twenty-four becomes the pieces two zero two and four.

38
00:02:50,495 --> 00:03:02,639
The long number one two three four five six seven eight nine zero one two three is chopped into one two three, four five six, seven eight nine, zero one two, and three.

39
00:03:02,919 --> 00:03:04,791
Pi is split too.

40
00:03:05,071 --> 00:03:12,127
So the model sees a number as a sequence of three-digit chunks, and which chunk boundaries you get depends on where the number starts.

41
00:03:12,407 --> 00:03:16,247
That is one reason arithmetic is awkward for language models.

42
00:03:17,047 --> 00:03:23,695
This is the same greeting, hello world, in English, Hindi, Japanese, Arabic and Russian.

43
00:03:23,975 --> 00:03:25,847
Sixty-three characters.

44
00:03:26,127 --> 00:03:30,615
On the newer o200k vocabulary it is twenty-one tokens.

45
00:03:30,895 --> 00:03:34,519
Now I switch to the older cl100k vocabulary.

46
00:03:34,799 --> 00:03:38,303
The same text is forty-one tokens, almost double.

47
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:42,734
The newer vocabulary was trained to cover more languages efficiently.

48
00:03:43,014 --> 00:03:46,854
The older one splits non-English text into many small pieces.

49
00:03:47,134 --> 00:03:49,007
For you that means two things.

50
00:03:49,286 --> 00:03:55,766
Text in some languages costs more, because you pay per token, and it uses up more of the context window.

51
00:03:56,046 --> 00:04:02,190
The comparison between the two vocabularies shows it is a design choice, and that the newer one improved on it.

52
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:04,863
Emoji are another case.

53
00:04:05,142 --> 00:04:13,854
Pizza and robot, plus a person with a skin tone and a laptop, come out as sixteen tokens on the new vocabulary and twenty-one on the old one.

54
00:04:14,134 --> 00:04:23,470
Each emoji is stored as several bytes, and the tokenizer splits them into byte fragments, so a single emoji can take two or three tokens.

55
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:26,798
At the bottom, the app turns the count into money.

56
00:04:27,078 --> 00:04:34,710
At the default price of two dollars fifty per million input tokens, a short line like this costs a tiny fraction of a cent.

57
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:39,718
It adds up only at scale, and it scales with tokens, not characters.

58
00:04:40,518 --> 00:04:44,406
That the answer to the strawberry question isn't about intelligence at all.

59
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:46,582
It is about the input format.

60
00:04:46,862 --> 00:04:50,318
The model isn't bad at counting; it never saw the letters.

61
00:04:50,598 --> 00:04:57,894
Also how much the vocabulary matters: the same greeting costs about half as many tokens with the newer tokenizer.

62
00:04:58,694 --> 00:05:01,214
This tool shows tokenisation only.

63
00:05:01,494 --> 00:05:08,478
It doesn't run a model, so it can't prove why a given model fails at a given task; it shows what the input looks like.

64
00:05:08,758 --> 00:05:11,854
It supports two OpenAI vocabularies.

65
00:05:12,134 --> 00:05:21,206
Other model families, like Claude, Llama or Gemini, use different tokenizers and would split the same text differently, and I have not compared them.

66
00:05:21,486 --> 00:05:25,590
The cost panel uses a price you can edit, not a live price list.

67
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:30,934
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

68
00:05:31,734 --> 00:05:40,518
Next I'd add more tokenizers side by side, so you can compare several model families on the same sentence, and show the token IDs next to the chips.

69
00:05:40,798 --> 00:05:43,702
A context-window meter would also be a good addition.

70
00:05:43,982 --> 00:05:48,470
The code is in the description, with no API key and no cost.

71
00:05:48,750 --> 00:05:56,622
If this changed how you see a chatbot's mistakes, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me what text I should put through the x-ray.
